WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.755
TypeScript services sometimes need
capabilities that live in external programs:

2
00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:08.057
document conversion, media processing,
archive inspection,

3
00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:12.028
version-control operations, compilers, or
operating-system utilities.

4
00:00:12.108 --> 00:00:15.390
Launching such a program crosses a
significant trust boundary.

5
00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:19.543
The child process is independent code with
access inherited from the parent and

6
00:00:19.623 --> 00:00:23.879
behavior defined by its executable,
arguments, environment, working directory,

7
00:00:23.959 --> 00:00:26.329
input files, and operating-system
identity.

8
00:00:26.409 --> 00:00:30.725
A secure design accounts for all of those
inputs rather than treating process launch

9
00:00:30.805 --> 00:00:32.386
as one harmless function call.

10
00:00:32.466 --> 00:00:36.539
Command injection occurs when external
data changes the meaning of an intended

11
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:38.929
command instead of remaining ordinary
data.

12
00:00:39.009 --> 00:00:41.258
Shell metacharacters are one visible form,

13
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.587
but the deeper problem is loss of
structure.

14
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:47.315
A caller might influence the executable,
add a target-program option,

15
00:00:47.395 --> 00:00:49.826
select a configuration file, redirect
output,

16
00:00:49.906 --> 00:00:53.067
or alter execution through environment and
directory state.

17
00:00:53.147 --> 00:00:55.275
These failures can occur without a shell.

18
00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:58.699
Argument separation prevents
shell-language interpretation,

19
00:00:58.779 --> 00:01:02.304
yet the selected program still interprets
its own argument grammar.

20
00:01:02.384 --> 00:01:04.694
The useful review questions are
structural.

21
00:01:04.774 --> 00:01:08.604
Determine which executable can run,
whether any shell parses the request,

22
00:01:08.684 --> 00:01:12.271
which argument positions may vary, and
where their values originate.

23
00:01:12.351 --> 00:01:16.241
Then examine the inherited environment,
current directory, credentials,

24
00:01:16.321 --> 00:01:19.908
file and network access, runtime,
concurrency, and captured output.

25
00:01:19.988 --> 00:01:22.663
Escaping one string cannot answer those
questions.

26
00:01:22.743 --> 00:01:26.572
It is also platform-specific because
Windows and POSIX process creation,

27
00:01:26.652 --> 00:01:29.266
shell quoting, and executable resolution
differ.

28
00:01:29.346 --> 00:01:33.359
The governing principle is that
application code owns the process structure.

29
00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:37.511
External input may select a narrowly
defined operation or fill a validated data

30
00:01:37.591 --> 00:01:40.753
position, but it does not become a general
command interface.

31
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.811
A storage identifier can map to a
server-controlled file,

32
00:01:43.891 --> 00:01:46.384
and a format choice can map to one
supported flag.

33
00:01:46.464 --> 00:01:50.597
The application selects the executable,
argument layout, context, privilege,

34
00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:51.285
and limits.

35
00:01:51.365 --> 00:01:55.621
This model turns process execution into a
small service capability whose behavior

36
00:01:55.701 --> 00:01:58.740
remains predictable when clients submit
unexpected data.
